WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.640
The UK and the European Union wrangled for&nbsp;
months over a deal that was supposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:10.240
regulate their post-Brexit trade relationship.&nbsp;
But almost a year after it went into force,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:15.520
the squabbling continues. French fishermen say&nbsp;
London's not honoring its side of the bargain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.800
by refusing to grant access to its waters -&nbsp;
and now Paris is threatening to take action.&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.560
The Jeremy-Florent is returning to harbor after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:28.320
a night out in the Channel - it&nbsp;
has not been a successful trip.&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:36.320
"The whole catch: There are about 20 kg of Dover&nbsp;
sole back there, and all these are dogfish;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:40.080
they don't really sell; they're worth&nbsp;
no more than 20 to 50 cents per kilo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:46.800
so it's nothing, and a few crabs. All this&nbsp;
stuff in the nets it's worth nothing."&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.080
The one box of good fish won't even&nbsp;
cover the costs of the boat's fuel.&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.840
Back at sea, Christophe shows us how limited&nbsp;
his fishing grounds have become since Brexit:&nbsp;

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.120
"This whole part there - we can't work anymore,&nbsp;
all that is left is this small bit here where we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:08.480
can work. It's nothing, only half is left. This&nbsp;
whole zone where we worked before we don't have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.360
access to. And the British - they'll pursue us if&nbsp;
we enter their waters. I don't have a license."&nbsp;

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.400
He's not the only one.
47 small boats applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.360
to keep fishing in UK waters post-Brexit,&nbsp;
but the UK side granted just 12 licenses.&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:28.960
"Here it's really a total loss. Since January&nbsp;
the small boats haven’t been allowed to enter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:34.800
Britain's 12 mile zone. We accuse them of not&nbsp;
respecting the agreement they signed, which says&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:39.840
access to the waters should be preserved. And&nbsp;
now they've added many criteria to block people."&nbsp;

00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:45.771
Paris has promised to fight back.
"The British side today obviously does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.771 --> 00:01:48.480
want to respect the agreement as it was signed.&nbsp;
There is now a great effort from the French side&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:53.840
with the EU Commission to move forward quickly&nbsp;
on this issue that has gone on for too long."&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:59.280
Here at Boulogne's fish market where the daily&nbsp;
catch is sold, families are feeling the squeeze:&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.760
"The ships can't go there any&nbsp;
more, it creates problems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.200
Less fishing, less fish, less money, less sales."&nbsp;

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:17.200
"We have less flat fish, things like plaice&nbsp;
and Dover sole are difficult to get. It's a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:24.160
catastrophe. With Brexit we can't go where we&nbsp;
did before, so we catch less fish. Last week&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:29.920
we had hoped we would get a license, but no. I&nbsp;
don't know what we'll do. Maybe go on strike?"&nbsp;

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:35.840
Some fishermen here have even threatened to block&nbsp;
the Channel tunnel connecting France and Britain.&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:41.280
Jeremy Lhomel followed in his father's&nbsp;
footsteps in becoming a fisherman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.920
but now he fears the line stops here.&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:52.080
"I'm not working more than 6 months per year,&nbsp;
this can't go on, it's over. Now the season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:55.360
for the whelks is beginning, and they live&nbsp;
in British waters and we can't go there.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.240
But hope keeps us alive; we're waiting&nbsp;
another week for news from our government.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:08.400
We'll see."
Batisse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:11.440
is the traditional symbol of&nbsp;
Boulogne's fishermen – the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:17.840
future he's looking out on has&nbsp;
never seemed more uncertain.

